            "typeClass": "compound",
            "value": [
              {
                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "This interview study is part of research project Protein Transition 2.0. This project studies novel dairy proteins produced via cellular agriculture, in light of the protein transition. Namely, animal proteins from livestock farming are not sustainable, while plant proteins cannot yet offer the same taste and nutritional value for human consumption. Cellular agriculture represent a major revolution, as it can efficiently meet the high demand for proteins with a high sensory and nutritional quality and a low environmental footprint. To convert these new proteins into attractive food products, technology development is still needed. In order to achieve large-scale production, the Protein Transition 2.0 project works on the application of scalable key technologies for downstream processing, improve the functionality of the proteins for food applications, as well as go through regulatory requirements and safety analyses.\n\nThe current interview study focusses on the regulatory procedure to get cellular agriculture-produced dairy proteins to the market. Producing dairy products via cellular agriculture, either via biomass fermentation or precision fermentation, is a rather new development and these products have not yet been marketed in the European Union. Therefore, risk assessment is required as one of the steps to get access to the market, as is laid down in the General Food Law. Risk assessment is a purely scientific evaluation to assure all food on the European market is safe for human consumption. It is performed by the European Food Safety Authority (EFSA), which is an independent agency that consists of panels of experts in the field of food and health. When a company requests market entrance for a new protein and the relevant EFSA panel comes to a positive risk assessment, the European Commission in collaboration with the member states are responsible for the risk management, where it is decided whether or not the new protein is allowed on the market. \nThe current study focusses on the first step of the scientific risk assessment. Since cellular agriculture-produced dairy proteins are a new development and the proteins are more complex compared to other novel foods, it is expected that the duration of the risk assessment is longer and more complex, for all involved parties. Additionally, the development of cellular agriculture-produced dairy proteins create an interesting tension point between sustainable innovations and food safety. Namely, sustainable food innovations are useful and desired in light of sustainability, but food safety remains the main goal of EU food law.\nThe objective of the current interview study is to gain insight in how the risk assessment procedure of cellular agriculture-produced dairy proteins currently works in practice and how it is perceived by the different stakeholders. The stakeholders are divided into three groups: (1) food business operators developing these alternative proteins and developing the required risk assessment dossier; (2) consultants who help food business operators with building and submitting a risk assessment dossier; and (3) EFSA panel members involved in conducting the risk assessment of cellular agriculture-produced dairy proteins. By this means, we will use the protein transition as a critical case to theorise how regulatory risk assessment systems adapt to sustainability-driven socio-technical transitions."
                }
              }
            ]
          },
